<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mir Jumla II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1591">1591</a>–<a href="/years/1663">1663</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Mir Muhammad Said Ardestani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mir Jumla II was a Persian-born merchant, general and statesman who served the Golconda Sultanate and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. Made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, he invaded Cooch Behar and the Ahom kingdom in 1661–1663, occupied the Ahom capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhgaon">Garhgaon</a> and imposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ghilajharighat">Treaty of Ghilajharighat</a>, but his army was ruined by disease and floods and he died on the return march in 1663.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mir-jumla-ii.jpg" alt="Mir Jumla II" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mir_Jumla_-_peinture_-_btv1b52505099t.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mir Jumla was born Mir Mohammad Sayyid Ardistani in Safavid Iran in 1591, the son of Mirza Hazaru, a poor oil merchant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>. He learned to read and write, which probably led to work as a clerk for a diamond merchant with connections to the Golconda Sultanate, a region known for its diamond mines. He may have reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> in 1630, though some scholars suggest 1615 or 1620. There he built a diamond business and a maritime trade, and gifts to the Qutb Shahi sultan, among them cabinets from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and elephants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>, won him a place at court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the 1630s Mir Jumla became Sar-i-Khail and administered trade at the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machilipatnam">Masulipatnam</a>, where his strict collection of dues angered the English company, and he rose to be chief minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>. By the 1640s his own fleets sailed to Arakan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>, Melaka, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-abbas">Bandar Abbas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>, and the historian Narayan Sarkar described his trade as a competitor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>.</p><p>From April 1642 he led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> armies into Karnatak, taking forts in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nellore">Nellore</a> plains and Udayagiri, besieging the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlement at Pulicat and, in 1650, capturing Gandikota with the help of European gunners. He melted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> idols taken from temples to cast artillery. When the sultan demanded a share of his spoils and plotted to seize and blind him, he opened a secret correspondence with Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> and entered Mughal service. After Aurangzeb's accession he pursued the rebel prince Shah Shuja, defeated at the Battle of Khajwa, and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> on 9 May 1660, by which time Shuja had fled to Arakan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As subahdar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> he restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> as the capital in place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajmahal">Rajmahal</a> and dismissed dishonest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazi">qazis</a>. His expedition against the Ahom kingdom took 12,000 cavalry, 30,000 infantry and 323 ships and boats crewed partly by Portuguese, English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> sailors. Hunger and night attacks during the rains cost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> two thirds of the army, and the peace bound the Ahom king to an indemnity, a yearly tribute of 20 elephants and the cession of the land from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a> to the Manas river.</p><p>Mir Jumla died on the march back on 30 March 1663 and was buried on a hillock at Mankachar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, where his tomb survives. The traveller François Bernier wrote that his death was &quot;a great sensation throughout the Indies&quot;. Parts of the roads, bridges and forts he built around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> still stand.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mir Jumla II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1662 the Mughal governor of Bengal, <b>Mir Jumla</b>, invaded, occupied the capital Garhgaon and forced Jayadhwaj Singha to accept the harsh Treaty of Ghilajharighat in 1663, but disease and monsoon floods ruined the Mughal army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A few merchants crossed into the imperial service, as the Persian diamond trader <b>Mir Jumla II</b> did, rising to be a Mughal general and governor of Bengal; extortion by officials or a raid could ruin a house.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1637" aria-label="Isfahan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5876 977 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mir Jumla II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6326 1247h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6326 1247h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1637">What was here in 1637? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
